DESCRIPTION:It is easy to see and feel: Lindy Hop\, Authentic Jazz\, and ma
 ny Cuban dances are all deeply rooted in African culture. Their grounded\, 
 rhythmic\, and at-ease character\, their freedom and naturalness of movemen
 t\, strongly represent this heritage. The cultural melting pot of late 1920
 s New York brought together musicians and composers from Harlem\, Cuba\, an
 d Puerto Rico\, all influencing each other\, offering and creating a rich l
 andscape of dance music including swing. Between the 1930s and 50s\, New Yo
 rk's many ballrooms- like the Savoy- were cooking with various styles of mu
 sic and dance\, and the old-timers\, when asked about these times\, state t
 hat “everybody danced everything”.\n\nGet your dose of Caribbean ease and s
 winging happiness with live swing music as well as DJed Cuban and swing mus
 ic from the 1930s\, 40s and 50s.\n\nHere we are again on the fine floor at 
 Nou Tango. The home of one of the best dancefloors in Berlin\, Nou is extre
 mely cozy\, with easy ambiance and a relaxed atmosphere. Dance and enjoy th
 e glorious parquet floor\, or kick back with a glass of wine and enjoy the 
 view.\n\n21:00 Dancefloor opens\n21:30 Cuban dance performance by Lisandra 
 Cervantes\, followed by a short animation\n21:45-23:30 Live music with Joha
 nnes Ballestrem and Band\n23:30-close DJ\n\nThe band:\nJohannes von Ballest
 rem - piano\nMarkus Ehrlich - saxophone\nSidney Werner - bass\nMatt Griffit
 hs - drums\n\nParty entry 8-10€\, sliding scale.\n\nkeep swingin'! berlinho
 p.com\n\nhttps://www.facebook.com/events/1043823765981487/
LAST-MODIFIED:20200311T235949Z
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:CANCELLED Uptown Lodown: Havana in Harlem II
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
