DESCRIPTION:It is easy to see and feel: the Lindy Hop and Cuban Rumba both 
 are deeply rooted in African culture. Their grounded\, rhythmic and at ease
  character as well as freedom and naturalness of movement strongly represen
 t this heritage. And furthermore\, the cultural melting pot of late 1920’s 
 New York brought together musicians and composers from Harlem\, Cuba and Pu
 erto Rico\, all influencing each other\, offering and creating a rich lands
 cape of dance music\, including swing. In the 1930s to 50s\, New York's man
 y ballrooms like the Savoy were cooking with various styles of music and da
 nce\, and old-timers claim that “everybody danced everything”.<br><br>Get y
 our dose of Caribbean ease and swinging happiness with live swing music as 
 well as DJed Cuban and swing music from the 1930s\, 40s and 50s.<br><br>Her
 e we are again on the fine floor at Nou Tango. The home of one of the best 
 dancefloors in Berlin\, Nou is extremely cozy\, with easy ambiance and a re
 laxed atmosphere. Dance and enjoy the glorious parquet floor\, or kick back
  with a glass of wine and enjoy the view.<br><br>21:00 Dancefloor opens<br>
 21:30 Cuban Rumba performance by Lisandra Cervantes\, followed by a short R
 umba animation<br>21:30-23:30 Live music with the Igor Spallati Quartet<br>
 23:30-close DJ<br><br>Party entry 8-10€\, sliding scale.<br><br>The Igor Sp
 allati Quartet are:<br>James Scannell - sax\, clarinet<br>Christopher Colaç
 o - piano<br>Igor Spallati - bass<br>Jordan Dinsdale - drums<br><br><a href
 ="https://www.facebook.com/events/369945013677751/">https://www.facebook.co
 m/events/369945013677751/</a><br><br>keep swingin'! <a href="http://berlinh
 op.com">berlinhop.com</a>
LAST-MODIFIED:20190920T121646Z
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Uptown Lodown: Havana in Harlem
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
